#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048826 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048678 :topic/math-academy-id 2225 :topic/title "Introduction to Systems of Linear Equations"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050622 :topic/math-academy-id 356 :topic/title "Solving Two-Variable Equations"}] :topic/id #uuid "c375d3cc-e398-5a47-a69f-ddc853431752" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073752 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073445 :question/id #uuid "e9aae52c-65f3-5dff-bc6b-a530f8c7acc1" :question/math-academy-id "e-13009" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y=4 \\\\ x+2y=16\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. It doesn't matter which equation we pick first, so we choose the easiest one. In this case, the first equation looks like the easiest.\n\nUsing the first equation, we can isolate $x$ by adding $y$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y & =4 \\\\ x & =4+y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we replace $x$ with $4+y$ in the second equation and solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\tx+2y &=16 \\\n\t(4+y) + 2y &=16\\\n\t4+3y &=16\\\n\t3y &=12\\\n\ty &=4\n}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $y=4$ into $x=4+y$, then we can find the value of $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\tx &= 4 + y \\\n\t   &= 4 + (4) \\\n\t   &=8\n}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x = 8$ and $y = 4$, which can be written as $(8, 4).$"} :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Linear Equations Using Substitution"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073753 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073446 :question/id #uuid "707504e8-a367-5dc2-a663-371a9dcf7224" :question/math-academy-id "e-13010" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2u+9v-6=0 \\\\ 2u+6v-8=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Sometimes, it's handy to assign a number to each equation in a system, so that we can refer to them more easily:\n![](images/77/77459b19bb1fd1f28e05e6f613e4e49af34595eae33eeeca584d65a289f7ea2c.svg)\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $\\color{blue}(2)$, we can solve for $u$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t2u+6v-8 &= 0 \\\n2u+6v &= 8 \\\n2u &= 8 - 6v \\\nu &= \\dfrac{8-6v}{2} \\\nu &= 4 - 3v\n}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $u=4-3v$ into equation $\\color{red}(1)$ and solve for $v\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t2u+9v-6 &= 0 \\\n\t2(4-3v)+9v-6 &= 0 \\\n\t8-6v+9v-6 &= 0 \\\n2+3v &= 0 \\\n3v &= -2 \\\nv &= -\\dfrac{2}{3}\n}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $v=-\\dfrac{2}{3}$ into equation $\\color{red}(1)$, then we can find the value of $u\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t2u+9v-6 &= 0 \\\n\t2u+9\\left(-\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right) -6 &= 0 \\\n\t2u-6-6 &= 0 \\\n\t2u-12 &= 0 \\\n\t2u &= 12 \\\n\tu &= 6\n}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $u=6$ and $v=-\\dfrac{2}{3}$, which can be written as $\\left(6,-\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right).$"} :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Challenging System of Linear Equations Using Substitution"}] :topic/math-academy-id 487 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048216 :topic/math-academy-id 5135 :topic/title "Proving Perpendicular Line Theorems"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048646 :topic/math-academy-id 6305 :topic/title "Surface Areas of Combined Prisms"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048873 :topic/math-academy-id 6280 :topic/title "Determining Unknown Parameters in Exponential Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048975 :topic/math-academy-id 6252 :topic/title "Linear Functions Containing Unknown Parameters"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048991 :topic/math-academy-id 5271 :topic/title "Further Proving Alternate Angle Theorems"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049144 :topic/math-academy-id 1255 :topic/title "Cartesian Equations of Parametric Curves"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049198 :topic/math-academy-id 890 :topic/title "Modeling Using Systems of Linear Equations"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049869 :topic/math-academy-id 408 :topic/title "Calculating the Intersection of Two Lines"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050326 :topic/math-academy-id 2026 :topic/title "Moments and Center of Mass"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050365 :topic/math-academy-id 1354 :topic/title "Properties of Parallelograms"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050564 :topic/math-academy-id 1081 :topic/title "Systems of Linear Equations With Decimal Coefficients"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050661 :topic/math-academy-id 1639 :topic/title "Complementary Angles"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050754 :topic/math-academy-id 6207 :topic/title "Non-Adjacent Complementary Angles"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050788 :topic/math-academy-id 6156 :topic/title "Solving Systems of Linear Equations by Identifying Structure"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050926 :topic/math-academy-id 1045 :topic/title "Systems of Linear Equations With Fractional Coefficients"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050964 :topic/math-academy-id 1062 :topic/title "Expressing Rational Functions with Repeated Factors as Sums of Partial Fractions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050974 :topic/math-academy-id 5137 :topic/title "Proving Consecutive Angle Theorems"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050984 :topic/math-academy-id 410 :topic/title "Solving Systems of Nonlinear Equations"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051014 :topic/math-academy-id 1105 :topic/title "Linear Combinations of Vectors and Their Properties"}] :topic/title "Solving Systems of Equations by Substitution"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049360 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049715 :topic/math-academy-id 1103 :topic/title "Parallel Vectors"}] :topic/id #uuid "eed250d1-ce5b-5073-a193-140fd0562d65" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096813 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096447 :question/id #uuid "2a61b115-4e26-5966-b2a9-d2fedef6b728" :question/math-academy-id "e-4282" :question/problem "The vector $\\mathbf{a}$ has magnitude $3$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$." :question/worked-solution "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$, we divide $\\mathbf{a}$ by its length:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=\\dfrac{\\mathbf{a}}{\\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, \\mid}=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\mathbf{a}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Unit Vector Parallel to a Given Vector"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096814 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096493 :question/id #uuid "6dfe09f0-30e2-53bc-a8f6-d7e4a3b8232a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5181" :question/problem "![](images/d3/d3042242136b82b867b8bd7911761ee38318924df0c9979488d20f04117c45d9.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf a$." :question/worked-solution "![](images/b3/b316448c885a20a7ed60871ce0f1a4dc4f77bd4658dc6eda3407fb3c89a088cb.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have\n\n$$\n\\angle AHB = 90^\\circ\n$$\n\n, $AH=3$ and $BH=2$. So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^{2} + BH^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3^{2} + 2^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{9 + 4} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{13}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$ is $\\sqrt{13}$. Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{1}{\\mid a \\mid} \\cdot a \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Unit Vector Parallel to a Given Vector in a Diagram"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096815 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096448 :question/id #uuid "23d9a601-974b-51c2-a6ea-0ebadde19efc" :question/math-academy-id "e-4283" :question/problem "The vector $\\mathbf{a}$ is directed horizontally to the right and has magnitude $10$, while the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ is directed vertically down and has magnitude $20$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a+b}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $\\mathbf{a+b}$. According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}$ goes from the tail of $\\mathbf{a}$ to the head of $\\mathbf{b}$:\n\n![](images/32/32c48d8c19fc68fbc172b240ded922f3e2ebc12804411c318ff63fe999aaedcc.png)\n\nTo find the unit vector corresponding to $\\mathbf{a+b}$, we have to divide by the magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{a + b}{\\mid a + b \\mid}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the magnitude, first notice that we obtain a right triangle. So, we can use the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}a + b & ^{2} &= & a & ^{2} +\\left.\\end{vmatrix}b\\right|^{2} \\\\\n&= 10^{2} + 20^{2} \\\\\n&= 100 + 400 \\\\\n&= 500\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\mathbf{a+b} &= \\sqrt{500}=10\\sqrt{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{a + b}{\\mid a + b \\mid} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{10\\sqrt{5}}(a + b)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Unit Vector Parallel to a Vector Sum"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1104 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048816 :topic/math-academy-id 1165 :topic/title "Two-Dimensional Vectors Expressed in Component Form"}] :topic/title "Unit Vectors"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049481 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048593 :topic/math-academy-id 35 :topic/title "The Power Rule for Differentiation"}] :topic/id #uuid "f8a6d16e-1a41-5d40-8c3d-467ebdcc3782" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064167 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064038 :question/id #uuid "368cbbae-4eff-5eff-a8f3-d3e8359447a9" :question/math-academy-id "e-364" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int x^2 \\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the power rule with $n=2$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\int x^2 \\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac{x^{2+1}}{2+1} + C = \\dfrac{x^3}{3}+C.\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 3{x}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Antiderivative of a Monomial"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064168 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064039 :question/id #uuid "818f4fbe-e251-57bd-90ae-b6517c6f2f89" :question/math-academy-id "e-367" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of  $\\sqrt x.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the square root as a rational power, $\\sqrt x= x^{1/2}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{1/2+1}}{(\\frac{1}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/2}}{(\\frac{3}{2})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3/2})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{3/2-1} & = \\\\ {x}^{1/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Antiderivative of a Radical Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064169 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064040 :question/id #uuid "c25e6037-3900-5314-b9a9-262fef07061c" :question/math-academy-id "e-368" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = \\dfrac 1 {x^3}$ and that $F'(x) = f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if $F'(x) = f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac 1 {x^3}= x^{-3}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-3$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-3+1}}{-3+1}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{-2}}{2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Antiderivative of a Reciprocal Function"}] :topic/math-academy-id 308 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048442 :topic/math-academy-id 43 :topic/title "The Constant Multiple Rule for Indefinite Integrals"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050057 :topic/math-academy-id 283 :topic/title "The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus"}] :topic/title "The Antiderivative"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049715 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048882 :topic/math-academy-id 1101 :topic/title "The Magnitude of a Vector"}] :topic/id #uuid "0d3ad89a-14be-5528-a12e-4252d281243e" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096648 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096445 :question/id #uuid "069b2882-f4a9-589f-a903-4e89d077cd85" :question/math-academy-id "e-4279" :question/problem "Suppose, $\\mathbf{a} \\neq \\mathbf{0}$ and $\\mathbf{b} \\neq \\mathbf{0}$. Given that $7\\mathbf{a}+14\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\lambda \\left(3\\mathbf{a}+6\\mathbf{b} \\right)$ are equal vectors, find the scalar factor $\\lambda$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n$$\n7\\mathbf{a}+14\\mathbf{b}= \\dfrac{7}{3} (3\\mathbf{a}+6\\mathbf{b})\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lambda=\\dfrac{7}{3}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Vectors are Parallel"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096649 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096446 :question/id #uuid "d1329498-4413-5d87-a814-be0d57a3afd0" :question/math-academy-id "e-4280" :question/problem "The vector $\\mathbf a$ is shown in the diagram below. Draw the vectors $2\\mathbf a$ and $-3\\mathbf a$.\n\n![](images/a5/a5eaa8baaf303ecbfa6c0fb207d6b19836cc90fc331e238cd473109f44fddf80.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that both $2\\mathbf{a}$ and $-3\\mathbf{a}$ must be parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$.\n\nNow, for the vector $2\\mathbf{a}$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $2\\mathbf{a}$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$.\n- Vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $2\\mathbf{a}$ have the same direction since the factor $2$ is positive.\n\n![](images/41/41eba7e6c5bbe420418726bd45efa668dc6be5ff5144ba35d52b53a43925c333.png)\n\nNext, for the vector $-3\\mathbf{a}$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $3\\mathbf{a}$ is $3$ times the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$.\n- Vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $-3\\mathbf{a}$ have opposite directions since the factor $-3$ is negative.\n\n![](images/ed/eda3f2572d98ef1789292ec6d35569487b32efa459673dcb95f8fdd0d1f4ae5b.png)"} :knowledge-point/title "Creating Parallel Vectors"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096700 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096494 :question/id #uuid "11c09a26-0e83-5a77-b777-aea8a3594960" :question/math-academy-id "e-5183" :question/problem "Consider the diagram that is shown below. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\mathbf{b}=-\\mathbf{c}$\n2. $\\mathbf{e}=2\\mathbf{a}$\n3. $\\mathbf{a}=2\\mathbf{e}$\n\n![](images/95/95ae711c1ccbf7543e6d269aa8d817a66e2ac348113ea71f3a1babcbb23d1ca3.png)" :question/worked-solution "- Statement I is false. The vectors $\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ have different magnitudes. So, $\\mathbf{b} \\neq -\\mathbf{c}$.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{e}$ are parallel and have the same direction. Also, the magnitude of $\\mathbf{e}$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$. So, $\\mathbf{e}=2\\mathbf{a}$.\n- Statement III is false. The magnitude of $\\mathbf{e}$ is greater than the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$. So, $\\mathbf{a} \\neq 2\\mathbf{e}$.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parallel Vectors From a Diagram"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1103 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049360 :topic/math-academy-id 1104 :topic/title "Unit Vectors"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051014 :topic/math-academy-id 1105 :topic/title "Linear Combinations of Vectors and Their Properties"}] :topic/title "Parallel Vectors"}]}
